comments.text:
With the binding count made exact (bug 19158), the growth policy of
hashed environments can be improved.  The table currently grows by only
20% at a time, so it settles at one to two bindings per chain, and
every extra binding visited on a chain is usually a cache miss on a
cons cell somewhere in the heap, so the load factor sets the cost of a
lookup.  Filling an environment with a million bindings one at a time
moves each binding about 5.7 times on the way, and lookups in the
resulting table visit 1.74 bindings per hit.

This report carries two patches, applying in order on top of the patch
in bug 19158 (all generated against r90483; each applies with
patch -p0 from the top of a trunk checkout):

1. env-hash-resize.diff: R_HashResize() and R_HashFrame() rehash every
   name's characters with R_Newhashpjw(), although the hash is cached
   in the name's CHARSXP the first time it is computed.  Use the cached
   hash through hashIndex(), whose definition moves out of the
   USE_GLOBAL_CACHE section (it does not depend on the global cache,
   and R_HashResize() is compiled unconditionally); this also drops the
   dead MIKE_DEBUG blocks.

2. env-hash-growth.diff: a table holding more than 0.75 bindings per
   chain moves to the next of a ladder of primes near 1.618 times each
   power of two (2^5 to 2^30), about twice its size; a user-sized table
   moves to the first ladder size at least 1.5 times its own.  The
   global symbol cache grows through the same check.  The CHARSXP cache
   uses its own hash and power-of-two sizes, so it keeps its old growth
   trigger, now written out at its single call site.

The sizes matter.  R_Newhashpjw() is a base-16 polynomial of the
characters (h = (h << 4) + c, with the top nibble folded back) and the
index is the hash modulo the size, so a modulus near a small multiple
of a power of two folds the lattice of digit values onto itself.  A
first attempt doubled to 2n + 1, which from 29 gives 15 * 2^k - 1, and
lookups got slower: a million names of the form key<N> occupied 23% of
the chains where a uniform hash gives 40%, with twice the lookup cost.
Primes do not help by themselves -- 2097169, the first prime above
2^21, is just as bad, while 2000001 and 1000003 are fine.  The hash
itself cannot change, because hashed environments are serialized with
their chains in place and must stay readable, so only the sizes can.
Simulated occupancy relative to a uniform hash, a million names at load
0.5, six name shapes:

  sizes                    key<N>     v<N> rand  8 letters  pkg.fun_<N>  <N>        my_var_<N>_x
  2n+1 from 29, at 2^21    0.59       0.58       0.97       0.68         0.53       0.78
  prime above 1.618*2^k    0.97-1.08  0.99-1.06  1.00       0.95-1.08    0.96-1.09  1.00-1.04

The old growth by 1.2 produced irregular sizes and avoided the problem
by accident (a grown table of 864241 slots is at 0.88 of uniform).

Measured on aarch64 macOS, clang -O2, current trunk vs trunk with the
three patches, default settings:

  filling 1e6 bindings via list2env() into a default-size environment:
    1110 -> 1011 ns per binding (both dominated by interning the symbols)
  mget() of all 1e6 keys: 1010 -> 803 ns per lookup
  mget() of all 1e5 keys:   90 ->  83 ns per lookup
  bindings visited per hit: 1.74 -> 1.20
  final table: 864241 -> 1696649 slots (1.16 -> 0.59 bindings per chain)

With the symbol-table cost taken out of the fill benchmark (via an
enlarged symbol table; a separate topic), filling from the default size
drops from about 890 to 325 ns per binding, and filling a presized
table from 346 to 294.  attach() of a 200000-element list takes about
6% longer, as its presized table now resizes once on the way.  Package
loading and R startup are unchanged.  The table takes about 16 bytes
per binding instead of 4 to 8, against the 56 bytes of the binding's
cons cell.

